> +/**
> + * struct ctlv_cursor - One attr position in one message.

kernel-doc: the description of @msg says "core-private bookkeeping" and `@index_scope` says "core-private", while the closing paragraph says all members belong to the core. If every member is core-private, `struct ctlv_cursor` should be opaque to families or have its internals in `drivers/ctlv/internal.h`; exposing up/start/tree/node/depth/`index_*_scope` in an installed-for-drivers header freezes layout details nobody outside the core may touch. "Fixed-size" is only needed so it can live on the stack; an opaque embedded blob (or sizing macro) would achieve that without exposing field names. @attr is documented as `NULL` "when a lookup found nothing" - a reader/writer dual use of one struct with four reading-only/writing-only fields (parent vs up/start) is a union candidate or two types.

> + * @msg: the message this position is in, core-private bookkeeping
> + * @attr: the attr itself, or NULL when a lookup found nothing
> + * @parent: reading, the nest containing @attr, NULL at the root
> + * @up: writing, cursor of the nest containing @attr, NULL at the root. The
> + *	chain of them is the open-nest stack
> + * @start: writing, where @attr begins in the message. An append reaches what
> + *	it lengthens through the buffer rather than through @attr, that buffer
> + *	being the core's own
> + * @tree: schema tree the node belongs to, which is a fill's own tree for an
> + *	attr that arrived through one
> + * @node: index of the node within @tree
> + * @depth: nesting levels above this position, the root being zero
> + * @index_nest_scope: schema scope used to read members of the physical nest
> + * @index_scope: core-private schema scope containing this attr
> + *
> + * Fixed-size, so family code can put a child cursor on the stack. The members

The sentence "One direction at a time, a helper of the other direction refusing the cursor." is not a grammatical sentence and the kernel-doc blob is hard to parse in general. Rewrite in plain language.

> + * belong to the core: a family reaches a message through what was generated
> + * for the op it answers. One direction at a time, a helper of the other
> + * direction refusing the cursor.
> + */
> +struct ctlv_cursor {
> +	struct ctlv_msg *msg;
> +	const struct ctlv_attr *attr;
> +	const struct ctlv_attr *parent;
> +	struct ctlv_cursor *up;

`up` is a pointer to a caller-stack cursor (`struct ctlv_cursor *`) while the doc says nothing about lifetime: it is valid only while the child cursor is live on the same stack. Please document it, since the whole point is that families put children on the stack. Also nothing here enforces the depth limit against `CTLV_MAX_NEST_DEPTH` (depth is `u32` with 32 max): 32 * `sizeof(cursor)` (~72 bytes) = 2.3 KiB of stack in a recursive family; fine but should be called out.

> +	size_t start;
> +	const struct ctlv_schema_tree *tree;
> +	u32 node;
> +	u32 depth;
> +	u32 index_nest_scope;
> +	u32 index_scope;
> +};
> +
