Summary for 0002 (header-only; unused and not buildable on its own until patch 3):

1. `CTLV_ALIGN()` is evaluated in the argument's type and wraps for a `u32` length near `U32_MAX` (returns 0; the kernel itself uses ALIGN((`size_t`)len) so only userspace/latent users are exposed); `CTLV_ATTR()`/`CTLV_OP()` do not mask fields. These are public UAPI macros so they cannot be fixed later.
2. Wire-visible enums (`ctlv_unit`, `ctlv_schema_kind`) rely on implicit numbering while claiming ABI stability; enum min=1 rule (ENUM zero invalid) is undocumented in UAPI/ABI doc; reserved flag bits not stated.
3. `ctlv_family.h` is not self-contained: uses `ARRAY_SIZE` without `<linux/array_size.h>`, relies on `CTLV_OP_TYPE_*`/`CTLV_OP_AVAILABILITY_*` from the generated header (patch 3), `kobject.h` pulled only for two constants.
4. Core-private types (`ctlv_cursor` internals, `ctlv_input_ref`) and redundant API (`ctlv_action_fn` == `ctlv_query_fn`, `pre_post` bool duplicating pre/post pointers, unused forward declarations) exposed in family-facing headers; no kernel-doc on any family API struct.
5. The root family object is named plain `ctlv_family` (other families use `ctlv_<name>_family`) and its NS import requirement is undocumented.
6. Commit message overstates ("in-kernel API") and does not explain stubs; ioctl-number reserves 00-0F for one ioctl and uses a different email than the SoB.
